---
import ToolShell from '@/components/ToolShell.astro';
import NginxGenerator from '@/components/tools/NginxGenerator.astro';
import { localePath } from '@/consts';
---

<ToolShell
  lang="en"
  slug="nginx"
  title="nginx reverse-proxy config generator"
  description="Build a reverse-proxy server block with TLS, security headers, caching and rate limiting — and see which directives must live in the http context instead. Runs entirely in your browser."
>
  <NginxGenerator lang="en" />

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:2rem">
    <h2>Why this one splits the output in two</h2>
    <p>
      Most generators hand you one <code>server { }</code> block. But <code>limit_req_zone</code>,
      <code>proxy_cache_path</code>, <code>map</code> and <code>gzip</code> are only legal in the
      <code>http</code> context — put one inside a <code>server</code> block and nginx refuses to start
      with <em>"directive is not allowed here"</em>. That error is the single most common reason a
      copy-pasted nginx config does not come up, so the output above is deliberately two blocks and
      every item says which context it belongs to.
    </p>
    <p>
      The other thing worth reading before you paste: <code>add_header</code> is inherited only if the
      inner block adds none of its own. The moment you put an <code>add_header</code> inside a
      <code>location</code>, every header from the <code>server</code> block disappears for that location.
      That is how sites end up with HSTS on the homepage and nowhere else.
    </p>
    <p>
      Related reading: <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/nginx-cookbook/')}>the nginx cookbook</a>,
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/web-security-headers/')}>web security headers</a>,
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/http-security-headers-and-csp/')}>CSP in practice</a>,
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/nginx-proxy-cache/')}>proxy caching</a>,
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/rate-limiting/')}>rate limiting</a>, and
      <a href={localePath('en', '/posts/tls-1-3-handshake/')}>the TLS 1.3 handshake</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The output is checked with nginx itself: all four presets were fed to
      <code>nginx -t</code> in a container and each reports
      <code>syntax is ok / test is successful</code>. That check earned its keep — the first
      version of this generator never closed its <code>location</code> block, and
      <code>nginx -t</code> was what caught it. The only warning left is the documented
      <code>listen ... http2</code> deprecation.
    </p>
  </div>
</ToolShell>
